WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.408
- Welcome back everyone
to new Coffee Break here

00:00:02.408 --> 00:00:04.190
on my channel Firm Learning.

00:00:04.190 --> 00:00:06.540
As you know, I am Heinrich
and on my channel I try

00:00:06.540 --> 00:00:08.590
to help you to become successful

00:00:08.590 --> 00:00:10.920
in the first years of your career.

00:00:10.920 --> 00:00:12.580
And today I'm very excited

00:00:12.580 --> 00:00:14.630
about this episode because it is going to

00:00:14.630 --> 00:00:18.550
be an episode that is
absolutely packed with value.

00:00:18.550 --> 00:00:20.350
I have Anthony here with me today

00:00:20.350 --> 00:00:23.500
a colleague of me at Building
Radar, welcome Anthony

00:00:23.500 --> 00:00:25.290
- Good to be here, thanks for having me.

00:00:25.290 --> 00:00:27.610
- Awesome and Anthony is
not just any colleague

00:00:27.610 --> 00:00:29.990
but he's our Head of Product.

00:00:29.990 --> 00:00:31.930
He's actually based in the London office.

00:00:31.930 --> 00:00:34.210
We have a second
dependence there in London

00:00:34.210 --> 00:00:36.650
and he's visiting this week
here in Munich so I thought

00:00:36.650 --> 00:00:38.670
what better opportunity can there be

00:00:38.670 --> 00:00:40.180
to just do this together?

00:00:40.180 --> 00:00:43.030
Because I think your story
is very, very interesting,

00:00:43.030 --> 00:00:44.510
going to dive into that in a moment.

00:00:44.510 --> 00:00:45.710
What this episode is going

00:00:45.710 --> 00:00:48.420
to be all about is three main points.

00:00:48.420 --> 00:00:51.330
First, we are very much
looking to explore the

00:00:51.330 --> 00:00:53.420
whole motivation of doing an MBA.

00:00:53.420 --> 00:00:55.100
Why should you do an MBA?

00:00:55.100 --> 00:00:56.560
Why shouldn't you maybe do an MBA?

00:00:56.560 --> 00:00:59.310
So this whole idea of the
reasoning to pursue it

00:00:59.310 --> 00:01:01.670
and why you are the
perfect person for that.

00:01:01.670 --> 00:01:03.170
Let's talk about this in a moment.

00:01:03.170 --> 00:01:06.440
Second, we are also going to
talk about why maybe in the end

00:01:06.440 --> 00:01:09.150
of the day, you might
decide not to pursue it.

00:01:09.150 --> 00:01:11.080
What other opportunities
are there if you decide

00:01:11.080 --> 00:01:13.480
against the MBA and why it
might be a good decision to

00:01:13.480 --> 00:01:15.140
then also not do it in the end.

00:01:15.140 --> 00:01:16.620
And then last but not least,

00:01:16.620 --> 00:01:18.380
we're going to talk about transitioning

00:01:18.380 --> 00:01:22.650
from an engineering role to a tech role,

00:01:22.650 --> 00:01:24.100
to large tech company at any

00:01:24.100 --> 00:01:26.930
of these foreign companies and so on.

00:01:26.930 --> 00:01:30.180
Anthony, but maybe just in a nutshell,

00:01:30.180 --> 00:01:31.930
why are you the perfect person

00:01:31.930 --> 00:01:33.620
to talk about these things about now?

00:01:33.620 --> 00:01:36.190
- I guess it was a very funny story.

00:01:36.190 --> 00:01:37.210
When you joined the company

00:01:37.210 --> 00:01:39.840
I'd only not long been here myself.

00:01:39.840 --> 00:01:42.700
Found out that you had
done a MBA at INSEAD.

00:01:42.700 --> 00:01:45.940
In fact, the class that
I was supposed to join.

00:01:45.940 --> 00:01:47.980
For some of the reasons we'll going today

00:01:47.980 --> 00:01:50.330
why I didn't take the INSEAD route.

00:01:50.330 --> 00:01:52.640
But yeah hopefully I've got
some interesting insights.

00:01:52.640 --> 00:01:54.650
It's a very difficult decision for me.

00:01:54.650 --> 00:01:56.020
Maybe you can kind of share that with some

00:01:56.020 --> 00:01:58.500
of the views of the channel
that might help inform some

00:01:58.500 --> 00:02:00.020
of the hard decisions
they might need to make

00:02:00.020 --> 00:02:01.620
about their career development.

00:02:01.620 --> 00:02:03.610
- So let's just unpack
the what you said, right?

00:02:03.610 --> 00:02:06.340
So Anthony, you applied to INSEAD.

00:02:06.340 --> 00:02:08.480
So you did all the things
that are necessary to apply

00:02:08.480 --> 00:02:10.510
for an MBA, which is, I
mean quite a lot, right?

00:02:10.510 --> 00:02:13.680
So doing an MBA, doing
all the preparation work.

00:02:13.680 --> 00:02:16.090
You even told me that did
a C of A level one just

00:02:16.090 --> 00:02:17.340
to prep for that.

00:02:17.340 --> 00:02:19.870
You applied, you got in, you got the offer

00:02:19.870 --> 00:02:20.993
you actually already
accepted the offer right?

00:02:20.993 --> 00:02:23.247
You accepted.
- Pay the deposit

00:02:23.247 --> 00:02:25.013
- Paid the, how much was that
if you don't mind to share?

00:02:25.013 --> 00:02:27.623
- 7,000 euros, is it now.

00:02:27.623 --> 00:02:28.456
- 7,000 euros?

00:02:28.456 --> 00:02:30.860
So you paid all that and then you decided

00:02:30.860 --> 00:02:32.380
in the end not to do it.

00:02:32.380 --> 00:02:33.460
But let's dive into that

00:02:33.460 --> 00:02:34.610
'cause I think that's very interesting.

00:02:34.610 --> 00:02:35.730
Maybe you can share a little bit

00:02:35.730 --> 00:02:36.790
just more about your background.

00:02:36.790 --> 00:02:39.160
What did you do before all of this

00:02:39.160 --> 00:02:41.590
and what was your initial
motivation to pursue it?

00:02:41.590 --> 00:02:43.300
Why did you decide that
yes, actually it would

00:02:43.300 --> 00:02:46.970
be a great idea for me
personally to do an MBA.

00:02:46.970 --> 00:02:49.480
- So I started off, I
studied civil engineering

00:02:49.480 --> 00:02:53.090
at university, joined a
structural engineering company.

00:02:53.090 --> 00:02:55.098
It's very predictable career path.

00:02:55.098 --> 00:02:58.040
You know, could spend
my entire career there.

00:02:58.040 --> 00:02:59.990
I was designing skyscrapers.

00:02:59.990 --> 00:03:02.060
Moved out to China, more responsibility.

00:03:02.060 --> 00:03:04.660
I was quite ambitious
and it started to occur

00:03:04.660 --> 00:03:09.070
to me that how did I really
kind of get to the next level?

00:03:09.070 --> 00:03:10.824
I started to look at some

00:03:10.824 --> 00:03:14.130
of the senior leaders around me, some

00:03:14.130 --> 00:03:16.800
of the developer companies
that we were working with

00:03:16.800 --> 00:03:20.050
and I started to spot this MBA after many

00:03:20.050 --> 00:03:22.853
of their kind of names in the
brushes and things like that.

00:03:22.853 --> 00:03:24.530
That was the first time
I really started thinking

00:03:24.530 --> 00:03:26.850
about an MBA.

00:03:26.850 --> 00:03:28.000
The more I dug into it

00:03:28.000 --> 00:03:30.680
the more I kind of reflected
on what I wanted to do

00:03:30.680 --> 00:03:33.623
with my own career was
actually move into technology.

00:03:34.740 --> 00:03:37.210
I was reading a lot of
stuff in the news, things,

00:03:37.210 --> 00:03:38.977
start to wonder, "Hang on a second.

00:03:38.977 --> 00:03:41.137
"What if AI starts designing buildings?

00:03:41.137 --> 00:03:43.180
"Then what am I going to do?"

00:03:43.180 --> 00:03:45.037
Still early in my career.

00:03:45.037 --> 00:03:48.030
Had this kind of like
existential threat moment

00:03:48.030 --> 00:03:49.300
and figured out that yeah

00:03:49.300 --> 00:03:51.330
if somebody was gonna disrupt my industry

00:03:51.330 --> 00:03:53.950
then I'd be the one doing it.

00:03:53.950 --> 00:03:55.930
And that's kind of what led me to look

00:03:55.930 --> 00:03:58.690
at considering an MBA to
make that career transition

00:03:58.690 --> 00:04:00.570
into the tech sector.

00:04:00.570 --> 00:04:02.590
- You said one thing that
I think is very interesting

00:04:02.590 --> 00:04:05.020
and I know that also just in
the Firm Learning community

00:04:05.020 --> 00:04:06.740
there's always some debate
also in the comments

00:04:06.740 --> 00:04:07.860
if I talk about these things.

00:04:07.860 --> 00:04:09.030
You said that you, you know,

00:04:09.030 --> 00:04:11.040
you looked at your senior
management, your leadership,

00:04:11.040 --> 00:04:13.540
many people had MBAs there.

00:04:13.540 --> 00:04:14.850
What is your reflection that

00:04:14.850 --> 00:04:17.700
how important is it in a
more traditional company

00:04:17.700 --> 00:04:20.240
which I understand that was
your career at that time?

00:04:20.240 --> 00:04:23.500
How important is it still to have an MBA?

00:04:23.500 --> 00:04:25.150
And to what extent is your perception

00:04:25.150 --> 00:04:26.520
that this depends maybe also just

00:04:26.520 --> 00:04:29.970
on the region or can just shed
some light that into that?

00:04:29.970 --> 00:04:31.870
- Yeah, that's a really good question.

00:04:32.710 --> 00:04:33.600
I think in hindsight,

00:04:33.600 --> 00:04:38.060
I didn't really understand why
these senior people had MBAs.

00:04:38.060 --> 00:04:40.860
It was more like a
pattern that I'd spotted

00:04:40.860 --> 00:04:42.970
which kind of led me down, you know

00:04:42.970 --> 00:04:45.010
the path of exploring an MBA.

00:04:45.010 --> 00:04:45.843
I wouldn't even say

00:04:45.843 --> 00:04:50.843
that I knew how an MBA
would get me to that role.

00:04:50.870 --> 00:04:54.860
I just felt like it was an
avenue I needed to explore.

00:04:54.860 --> 00:04:57.410
And maybe in hindsight, not everybody

00:04:57.410 --> 00:04:59.700
in a senior leadership
position has an MBA.

00:04:59.700 --> 00:05:02.363
And so there are obviously
other paths to get there.

00:05:03.784 --> 00:05:06.790
And I think, yeah, the MBA was
really an opportunity for me

00:05:06.790 --> 00:05:11.090
to take some time with
like-minded ambitious individuals

00:05:11.090 --> 00:05:12.330
to find out what I actually
really wanted to do

00:05:12.330 --> 00:05:13.923
in my career long term.

00:05:15.520 --> 00:05:17.050
- And so then this was the journey.

00:05:17.050 --> 00:05:18.523
You decided to apply.

00:05:19.430 --> 00:05:21.040
Why did you go for INSEAD?

00:05:21.040 --> 00:05:25.130
What was appealing for you
for this specific program?

00:05:25.130 --> 00:05:29.040
- Yeah, INSEAD, I think one of
the, I mean a big plus point

00:05:29.040 --> 00:05:31.040
obviously it's a one year program

00:05:31.040 --> 00:05:32.870
and when you're thinking
about opportunity costs.

00:05:32.870 --> 00:05:34.963
I was already, you know, into my career.

00:05:36.290 --> 00:05:37.650
I guess the second
thing though actually is

00:05:37.650 --> 00:05:42.240
that I've since making that
decision lived in America

00:05:42.240 --> 00:05:43.073
for a few years

00:05:43.073 --> 00:05:45.980
and I've kind of really seen
a different side to America

00:05:45.980 --> 00:05:47.700
but maybe as somebody not being born

00:05:47.700 --> 00:05:50.050
in America had this kind of, oh, you know

00:05:50.050 --> 00:05:51.760
American MBA schools, they're only aware

00:05:51.760 --> 00:05:55.700
of American MBA schools and
being a European at heart

00:05:55.700 --> 00:05:57.540
I'm gonna go to the European one.

00:05:57.540 --> 00:05:59.670
And so INSEAD was high in the ranking

00:05:59.670 --> 00:06:02.830
but obviously also being in
Asia at the time, INSEAD having

00:06:02.830 --> 00:06:05.820
that Singapore campus was
a really big plus point

00:06:05.820 --> 00:06:07.293
for me personally, so.

00:06:08.440 --> 00:06:09.482
- So you apply to INSEAD

00:06:09.482 --> 00:06:10.650
and, I mean, I think I would

00:06:10.650 --> 00:06:11.820
share a similar rationale, right?

00:06:11.820 --> 00:06:12.880
So from my humble perspective,

00:06:12.880 --> 00:06:15.440
why might INSEAD be a good choice first

00:06:15.440 --> 00:06:17.030
if you don't see your future in the U.S.?

00:06:17.030 --> 00:06:20.130
Because I think indeed if
you aim to work in the U.S.

00:06:20.130 --> 00:06:21.830
the U.S. schools will always
have a better standing

00:06:21.830 --> 00:06:22.663
and a better reputation.

00:06:22.663 --> 00:06:24.450
I think that's one
important consideration.

00:06:24.450 --> 00:06:27.310
Second is that it's a
one year program, right?

00:06:27.310 --> 00:06:29.830
So if you think about ROI,
of course the one thing is

00:06:29.830 --> 00:06:32.580
that you invest twice the time.

00:06:32.580 --> 00:06:35.270
So you have twice the cost in
terms of tuition, living costs

00:06:35.270 --> 00:06:38.810
but also you have two years
of forgoing salary, right?

00:06:38.810 --> 00:06:41.760
Which I think many people
sometimes forget to include

00:06:41.760 --> 00:06:42.950
in the calculation.

00:06:42.950 --> 00:06:44.180
So yeah, I might be nice.

00:06:44.180 --> 00:06:46.120
And then of course
outside the U.S., I think,

00:06:46.120 --> 00:06:49.180
INSEAD may be close to London
Business School and so on,

00:06:49.180 --> 00:06:50.803
surely the number one program.

00:06:52.590 --> 00:06:54.450
So you applied, you even got in.

00:06:54.450 --> 00:06:58.430
Can you just walk us through
this application process?

00:06:58.430 --> 00:07:00.507
So what were all the things
that you did for that?

00:07:00.507 --> 00:07:02.010
And how did that feel like for you,

00:07:02.010 --> 00:07:04.860
someone coming from an
engineering background?

00:07:04.860 --> 00:07:08.533
- It was really hard to be quite frank.

00:07:09.600 --> 00:07:10.520
And I think actually looking

00:07:10.520 --> 00:07:12.230
at one of your recent videos, in fact

00:07:12.230 --> 00:07:13.830
you were talking about this kind

00:07:13.830 --> 00:07:16.710
of like the danger of over fixating

00:07:16.710 --> 00:07:18.430
on a very singular goal.

00:07:18.430 --> 00:07:21.570
And I decided I'd internalized
that it was INSEAD

00:07:21.570 --> 00:07:23.693
or nothing for various factors.

00:07:24.980 --> 00:07:27.510
It's really tough to
get those GMAT scores.

00:07:27.510 --> 00:07:31.200
I got something that I
thought might scrape me in.

00:07:31.200 --> 00:07:34.817
I spoke to the admissions
officer and she said,

00:07:34.817 --> 00:07:36.117
"If you think you can do better,

00:07:36.117 --> 00:07:37.810
"you should maybe try again."

00:07:37.810 --> 00:07:39.880
And I went and gave another shot.

00:07:39.880 --> 00:07:41.390
The, obviously the language, you know

00:07:41.390 --> 00:07:44.623
the additional language interview,
that was a big challenge.

00:07:45.970 --> 00:07:49.140
But yeah, just thinking back

00:07:49.140 --> 00:07:51.790
to those feelings like
waiting for the decision

00:07:51.790 --> 00:07:55.643
to come back, wondering whether
my GMAT score was enough.

00:07:56.933 --> 00:07:58.470
It really felt at that time

00:07:58.470 --> 00:08:00.820
that if I didn't get offered a place

00:08:00.820 --> 00:08:03.550
I wouldn't have known
what to have done next.

00:08:03.550 --> 00:08:06.420
It would've been, I feel like
such a big disappointment.

00:08:06.420 --> 00:08:09.040
I really didn't have a
backup plan at that time.

00:08:09.040 --> 00:08:12.830
And in retrospect it was foolish, I think,

00:08:12.830 --> 00:08:14.650
to think like that.

00:08:14.650 --> 00:08:15.690
- What would you do differently

00:08:15.690 --> 00:08:18.590
if you would face the
situation again today?

00:08:18.590 --> 00:08:22.560
- I think I needed to, I was
ultimately pursuing an MBA

00:08:22.560 --> 00:08:25.120
for a specific outcome and
I knew what the outcome was.

00:08:25.120 --> 00:08:26.010
I wanted to get

00:08:26.010 --> 00:08:29.430
from the engineering sector
into the tech sector.

00:08:29.430 --> 00:08:32.230
And I think I'd just become lost

00:08:32.230 --> 00:08:35.490
in the forums and, you
know, the process to think

00:08:35.490 --> 00:08:39.440
that an MBA was the only mechanism to get

00:08:39.440 --> 00:08:40.920
to where I wanted to go.

00:08:40.920 --> 00:08:43.830
And the reality was that
just simply wasn't true

00:08:43.830 --> 00:08:46.020
which I later found out.

00:08:46.020 --> 00:08:46.853
- Found out that.

00:08:46.853 --> 00:08:48.050
And it's interesting,
let's talk about that

00:08:48.050 --> 00:08:50.080
in a moment as well.

00:08:50.080 --> 00:08:51.870
I mean, you talked about
the GMAT in the beginning.

00:08:51.870 --> 00:08:53.290
We quickly talked about this deposit

00:08:53.290 --> 00:08:54.300
that you even needed to pay.

00:08:54.300 --> 00:08:56.220
I mean, you were really fully committed

00:08:56.220 --> 00:08:58.460
and you even joined, I mean,
you showed me some photos

00:08:58.460 --> 00:09:01.270
of pre MBA trips, right, that you joined.

00:09:01.270 --> 00:09:02.720
I mean, maybe just talk
a little bit about that.

00:09:02.720 --> 00:09:03.790
So how did that work?

00:09:03.790 --> 00:09:04.623
What did you do there?

00:09:04.623 --> 00:09:05.456
- That was fantastic.

00:09:05.456 --> 00:09:07.250
So I was living in Shanghai at the time.

00:09:08.460 --> 00:09:11.810
I was, I think I was in the
very first cohort accepted.

00:09:11.810 --> 00:09:14.170
And so, you know, the
school puts us in touch

00:09:14.170 --> 00:09:16.160
with the other people that
were accepted in our cities.

00:09:16.160 --> 00:09:18.190
And so lots of great nights

00:09:18.190 --> 00:09:21.673
out with all of the
Shanghai INSEAD candidates.

00:09:22.740 --> 00:09:23.720
And it was this kind

00:09:23.720 --> 00:09:26.080
of like camaraderie that
we were already developing.

00:09:26.080 --> 00:09:29.023
We're all so excited for where
we were gonna be next year.

00:09:30.170 --> 00:09:31.970
Yeah, it makes me really
actually think about,

00:09:31.970 --> 00:09:34.160
that's probably the thing
that makes me really

00:09:34.160 --> 00:09:35.910
realize what perhaps I missed out on

00:09:35.910 --> 00:09:38.400
by not actually going that route.

00:09:38.400 --> 00:09:42.483
But yeah, that was really
kind of fabulous time.

00:09:43.380 --> 00:09:44.980
INSEAD were fantastic in terms

00:09:44.980 --> 00:09:47.520
of inviting us on campus for like a,

00:09:47.520 --> 00:09:49.080
I think it was like a short
weekend where you could kind

00:09:49.080 --> 00:09:51.570
of really kind of get a
taste for what it was going

00:09:51.570 --> 00:09:52.403
to be like.

00:09:52.403 --> 00:09:54.600
I think that really
helped me mentally prepare

00:09:54.600 --> 00:09:57.280
for what it was going to be like.

00:09:57.280 --> 00:10:01.250
But yeah, in some cases you
are waiting maybe nine months

00:10:01.250 --> 00:10:02.330
for the program to start.

00:10:02.330 --> 00:10:04.990
That's a really exciting time.

00:10:04.990 --> 00:10:07.370
And yeah, it was trying to productively

00:10:07.370 --> 00:10:10.570
invest that excitement to
make sure that, you know

00:10:10.570 --> 00:10:11.470
I was gonna be as successful

00:10:11.470 --> 00:10:13.900
as possible when the program started.

00:10:13.900 --> 00:10:15.580
- That sounds great, right.

00:10:15.580 --> 00:10:16.690
And you did all that.

00:10:16.690 --> 00:10:19.010
You mentally prepared,
you visited the campus,

00:10:19.010 --> 00:10:20.670
you joined the pre MBA trips

00:10:20.670 --> 00:10:24.197
but still then there was
the point where you said,

00:10:24.197 --> 00:10:28.400
"For me at this point in time,
it's not the right decision."

00:10:28.400 --> 00:10:31.040
And again, can you unpack this as well?

00:10:31.040 --> 00:10:32.950
What led you to that realization?

00:10:32.950 --> 00:10:35.243
- Yeah, so it was very much unexpected.

00:10:36.350 --> 00:10:39.620
I was, again, I guess,
with all of that energy

00:10:39.620 --> 00:10:41.070
getting ready for the program

00:10:41.070 --> 00:10:46.070
I really started looking into
applying for internship roles.

00:10:47.070 --> 00:10:49.820
Because I didn't have any tech experience

00:10:49.820 --> 00:10:52.990
from my careers to date felt
like an internship would really

00:10:52.990 --> 00:10:54.870
kind of help me get that first job

00:10:54.870 --> 00:10:56.340
after the program finished.

00:10:56.340 --> 00:11:00.550
And so I blindly approached
this particular company.

00:11:00.550 --> 00:11:03.090
I discovered through the news

00:11:03.090 --> 00:11:07.130
which was a Google X
spinout company involved

00:11:07.130 --> 00:11:10.170
with automation of design
in the architecture space.

00:11:10.170 --> 00:11:12.320
It was, it had my name
written all over it.

00:11:13.260 --> 00:11:15.563
I reached out to them once, no response.

00:11:16.550 --> 00:11:19.530
Waited a couple months, gave
another shot in the dark

00:11:19.530 --> 00:11:21.540
and amazingly somebody got back to me

00:11:22.640 --> 00:11:24.650
and that essentially started a series

00:11:24.650 --> 00:11:27.250
of interviews with that company.

00:11:27.250 --> 00:11:32.250
I did an onsite interview as
well, and surprisingly, I just

00:11:32.990 --> 00:11:37.940
I wasn't expecting it, they
offered me a full-time role.

00:11:37.940 --> 00:11:41.170
And I was only asking for an internship.

00:11:41.170 --> 00:11:43.860
I think startups, they
have a different kind

00:11:43.860 --> 00:11:47.650
of timeframe of reference than
maybe the bigger companies.

00:11:47.650 --> 00:11:50.340
And they were like, look,
you know, we need you now.

00:11:50.340 --> 00:11:52.797
Like, and so, you know, I was saying,

00:11:52.797 --> 00:11:55.127
"Well, can I go and do this program first

00:11:55.127 --> 00:11:56.640
"and come back in a year?"

00:11:56.640 --> 00:11:59.100
And then startup might
not be around in a year

00:11:59.100 --> 00:12:01.400
or we might have been doing
something else in a year.

00:12:01.400 --> 00:12:04.660
And so I really felt like it
was a now or never opportunity.

00:12:04.660 --> 00:12:08.130
And yeah, it was very difficult to think

00:12:08.130 --> 00:12:10.720
about all of the things that
I would've missed out on going

00:12:10.720 --> 00:12:14.343
to INSEAD, the access to
like a lifetime network,

00:12:15.200 --> 00:12:18.380
You know, just the strengthening of my CV.

00:12:18.380 --> 00:12:19.580
But at the same time

00:12:19.580 --> 00:12:22.790
I already had this option here
to achieve my end objective

00:12:22.790 --> 00:12:24.800
which was to transition
into the tech industry.

00:12:24.800 --> 00:12:27.570
- Move to San Francisco, move
to the Silicon Valley right.

00:12:27.570 --> 00:12:29.160
To really get all this impressions

00:12:29.160 --> 00:12:30.510
- Exactly, exactly.

00:12:30.510 --> 00:12:32.960
And, you know, obviously,
yeah, there was the 7,000 Euro

00:12:32.960 --> 00:12:35.970
down payment and all of
the expectations I'd set

00:12:35.970 --> 00:12:39.120
with my friends and my now
wife, that this is what

00:12:39.120 --> 00:12:40.570
I was going to do for a year.

00:12:41.530 --> 00:12:44.660
But in the end, I think I just
had to be honest with myself

00:12:44.660 --> 00:12:48.270
that I was doing the MBA
for this particular outcome.

00:12:48.270 --> 00:12:50.050
And here I had a window of opportunity

00:12:50.050 --> 00:12:53.810
for this outcome and I had to take it.

00:12:53.810 --> 00:12:56.900
- And just for the uninitiated,
I mean, Google X is part

00:12:56.900 --> 00:12:57.780
of alphabet, right?

00:12:57.780 --> 00:13:00.180
So Google it's this
incubator where they spin

00:13:00.180 --> 00:13:02.060
off new innovative businesses

00:13:02.060 --> 00:13:04.370
and you had then the
opportunity to join one of them.

00:13:04.370 --> 00:13:06.710
So indeed you exactly like
the outcome that you wanted

00:13:06.710 --> 00:13:09.060
to get through the MBA
suddenly was presented

00:13:09.060 --> 00:13:12.650
to you just like that, just
by you taking the courage

00:13:12.650 --> 00:13:13.483
to apply, right.

00:13:13.483 --> 00:13:16.090
And I know like why didn't
you apply already before?

00:13:16.090 --> 00:13:17.540
I mean, that's the
obvious question, right?

00:13:17.540 --> 00:13:19.760
Like, why didn't you
already apply a year earlier

00:13:19.760 --> 00:13:21.960
before you even started
applying to the MBA?

00:13:21.960 --> 00:13:23.670
- It's a really good question.

00:13:23.670 --> 00:13:25.750
There was the reality is
there was nothing stopping me

00:13:25.750 --> 00:13:27.490
from doing that.

00:13:27.490 --> 00:13:30.230
I think again, in retrospect

00:13:30.230 --> 00:13:34.030
I like to think that
doing an MBA is a socially

00:13:34.030 --> 00:13:38.910
acceptable reason to totally
change your career trajectory.

00:13:38.910 --> 00:13:42.490
And I don't know why, you
know, maybe that fear that

00:13:42.490 --> 00:13:44.040
in the future, somebody's going to look

00:13:44.040 --> 00:13:46.730
at your CV and there'll
be some career transition

00:13:46.730 --> 00:13:48.380
that maybe doesn't make sense.

00:13:48.380 --> 00:13:51.380
But yeah, I think the MBA was the thing

00:13:51.380 --> 00:13:53.300
that really was just making me reflect.

00:13:53.300 --> 00:13:56.250
And as a result gave me the time

00:13:56.250 --> 00:13:58.810
and maybe motivation to
think about what I really

00:13:58.810 --> 00:14:00.890
wanted to do and then
start taking those actions

00:14:00.890 --> 00:14:02.620
I should have just taken earlier.

00:14:02.620 --> 00:14:04.980
- Earlier, I think that's fair right.

00:14:04.980 --> 00:14:07.620
And at least, and I mean,
you were maybe not lucky.

00:14:07.620 --> 00:14:09.390
Probably it is your
abilities and skills, right?

00:14:09.390 --> 00:14:11.490
So you were able to get that internship

00:14:11.490 --> 00:14:13.770
without the MBA or this position, actually

00:14:13.770 --> 00:14:15.930
for many people that
were also in my cohort

00:14:15.930 --> 00:14:19.390
or in the cohort that both
of us would've been part of

00:14:19.390 --> 00:14:21.350
these types of career
transition surely were one

00:14:21.350 --> 00:14:22.480
of the main motivations to do MBAs.

00:14:22.480 --> 00:14:24.470
And there were always these three things

00:14:24.470 --> 00:14:25.670
that people talked about.

00:14:25.670 --> 00:14:28.100
So transitions in regards to geography,

00:14:28.100 --> 00:14:31.040
in regards to industry or
in regards to function.

00:14:31.040 --> 00:14:34.200
This is what many, many
people were looking after.

00:14:34.200 --> 00:14:37.160
So doing a transition, at
least in one of these areas

00:14:37.160 --> 00:14:41.590
if not in two or even all
three, and for many, it worked.

00:14:41.590 --> 00:14:45.150
For some probably also that
didn't reach the goal post MBA.

00:14:45.150 --> 00:14:47.210
And this was, I think indeed also one

00:14:47.210 --> 00:14:48.250
of the points I tried to make

00:14:48.250 --> 00:14:49.980
in this other video the other week.

00:14:49.980 --> 00:14:51.621
I think just not very clever, right.

00:14:51.621 --> 00:14:53.630
Just to join, I mean,
just for this one purpose.

00:14:53.630 --> 00:14:55.920
I mean you, I think, had
a broader perspective.

00:14:55.920 --> 00:14:58.040
It's not that you wanted
to join just this one

00:14:58.040 --> 00:15:01.240
specific company, but rather
a sector, an industry.

00:15:01.240 --> 00:15:04.360
I think that's probably fair,
but some people are just also

00:15:04.360 --> 00:15:06.160
so tied up on some of these other elements

00:15:06.160 --> 00:15:07.610
that just doesn't make sense.

00:15:08.450 --> 00:15:11.080
But yeah, so it's, I think,
a very bold decision, right,

00:15:11.080 --> 00:15:12.627
to just say, "I cut the ties.

00:15:12.627 --> 00:15:15.237
"I invested all of this, I did all this

00:15:15.237 --> 00:15:17.397
"and I just do, take the other route."

00:15:18.400 --> 00:15:22.830
If you reflect on that today,
how do you perceive all

00:15:22.830 --> 00:15:24.300
of this that played out now in the end?

00:15:24.300 --> 00:15:26.463
I mean, was that the right
decision at the time?

00:15:28.500 --> 00:15:29.570
- I guess I'll never know.

00:15:29.570 --> 00:15:31.020
I think that's the reality.

00:15:31.020 --> 00:15:33.210
I don't have any regrets.

00:15:33.210 --> 00:15:37.260
Maybe if I had chosen the
INSEAD path, I don't know.

00:15:37.260 --> 00:15:39.870
I don't know how my life
could have been different

00:15:39.870 --> 00:15:43.090
but I think it was, again,
the reason why we, you know

00:15:43.090 --> 00:15:45.240
so fantastic when you joined the company.

00:15:45.240 --> 00:15:48.147
It was like, "Wow, maybe if
I had gone the INSEAD route,

00:15:48.147 --> 00:15:50.490
"I would've ended up back
here with Heinrich anyway."

00:15:50.490 --> 00:15:52.950
Like maybe these different paths take you

00:15:52.950 --> 00:15:54.740
to the same destination.

00:15:54.740 --> 00:15:57.637
And yeah, I just think
that that would, again

00:15:57.637 --> 00:16:00.570
it was just such a difficult
decision at the time.

00:16:00.570 --> 00:16:03.130
I really wasn't sure which route was going

00:16:03.130 --> 00:16:06.100
to have the highest payoff
long term in my career.

00:16:06.100 --> 00:16:10.910
I think that I went for
the short term opportunity

00:16:10.910 --> 00:16:14.680
versus the long term gains
that you almost guaranteed get

00:16:14.680 --> 00:16:17.890
with that MBA program
and the brand recognition

00:16:17.890 --> 00:16:19.100
and the access to the community.

00:16:19.100 --> 00:16:23.210
But again, it was a means to an end

00:16:23.210 --> 00:16:25.690
and there seemed like there was a shortcut

00:16:25.690 --> 00:16:26.860
to the end that

00:16:26.860 --> 00:16:28.430
I had to take.
- That makes sense.

00:16:28.430 --> 00:16:31.610
Let's maybe as a third
part of this video talk

00:16:31.610 --> 00:16:33.360
about just in general, this transition

00:16:33.360 --> 00:16:35.210
from engineering to tech.

00:16:35.210 --> 00:16:38.020
Because I mean, again
just also reading comments

00:16:38.020 --> 00:16:40.430
and listening to many
of you guys out there.

00:16:40.430 --> 00:16:41.263
I know that many

00:16:41.263 --> 00:16:43.230
of you also come from
a technical background.

00:16:43.230 --> 00:16:46.470
So rather than sense of
engineering than the tech industry

00:16:46.470 --> 00:16:48.650
and actually would love
to do this transition.

00:16:48.650 --> 00:16:51.410
Do you have any advice or
maybe also just reflections

00:16:51.410 --> 00:16:55.130
on your journey to share
what people can do?

00:16:55.130 --> 00:16:56.580
So an MBA can be an option.

00:16:56.580 --> 00:16:58.020
I think this is one of the learnings

00:16:58.020 --> 00:16:59.410
but doesn't need to be an option.

00:16:59.410 --> 00:17:00.790
So what is your take on this,

00:17:00.790 --> 00:17:03.030
on succeeding in this transition?

00:17:03.030 --> 00:17:04.200
- I think the reality is

00:17:04.200 --> 00:17:08.520
that the tech sector didn't exist maybe 15

00:17:08.520 --> 00:17:12.120
20 years ago in the form
that it exists today.

00:17:12.120 --> 00:17:15.340
And so it wasn't like
there were a million people

00:17:15.340 --> 00:17:17.890
all studying tech at university

00:17:17.890 --> 00:17:20.930
and now they all work in the tech sector.

00:17:20.930 --> 00:17:24.730
They came from other programs like finance

00:17:24.730 --> 00:17:27.490
or traditional engineering programs

00:17:27.490 --> 00:17:29.860
but also creative functions as well.

00:17:29.860 --> 00:17:33.220
And so, yeah, I think I had it in my head

00:17:33.220 --> 00:17:35.850
that I had have studied
software engineering

00:17:35.850 --> 00:17:37.400
and that it was too late already.

00:17:37.400 --> 00:17:39.200
And therefore I could
never get a seat, you know

00:17:39.200 --> 00:17:40.400
in the tech sector.

00:17:40.400 --> 00:17:43.320
But yeah, actually I'm really astonished

00:17:43.320 --> 00:17:45.670
at the the variety of backgrounds

00:17:45.670 --> 00:17:49.950
that people have coming
into the tech sector.

00:17:49.950 --> 00:17:51.130
And also the realization

00:17:51.130 --> 00:17:54.270
that not everybody at a tech
company writes software.

00:17:54.270 --> 00:17:57.250
There's people like yourself
involved with, you know

00:17:57.250 --> 00:18:01.810
how do we market and sell
the product, what I do now.

00:18:01.810 --> 00:18:04.843
Like how do we decide what to even build?

00:18:06.210 --> 00:18:07.043
I think coming

00:18:07.043 --> 00:18:09.460
from an engineering
background is a big plus point

00:18:10.620 --> 00:18:13.210
in the tech sector, because
at the end of the day

00:18:13.210 --> 00:18:15.440
you are working with software engineers

00:18:15.440 --> 00:18:18.140
and having that engineering background

00:18:18.140 --> 00:18:21.450
being able to empathize

00:18:21.450 --> 00:18:23.530
with the realities of building anything

00:18:24.530 --> 00:18:26.660
put me in a relatively strong position

00:18:27.740 --> 00:18:31.200
but also like the rise
of B2B software companies

00:18:32.460 --> 00:18:34.680
having a specific industry background

00:18:34.680 --> 00:18:37.120
and bringing that knowledge
of the construction sector

00:18:37.120 --> 00:18:38.440
which I had

00:18:38.440 --> 00:18:41.500
into a tech company helped
us build new tech products

00:18:41.500 --> 00:18:43.240
for the construction sector.

00:18:43.240 --> 00:18:44.073
And so yeah

00:18:44.073 --> 00:18:48.273
in a way it can actually be
a golden ticket to get in.

00:18:49.300 --> 00:18:51.600
- And actually, if you would be interested

00:18:51.600 --> 00:18:54.550
to join a tech company as well,
we here at Building Radar,

00:18:54.550 --> 00:18:56.290
we are currently hiring.

00:18:56.290 --> 00:18:57.810
There's several open positions.

00:18:57.810 --> 00:19:00.030
We will leave job posting links

00:19:00.030 --> 00:19:01.920
to that in the description.

00:19:01.920 --> 00:19:03.480
So feel free to check it out.

00:19:03.480 --> 00:19:04.570
Both actually for your team

00:19:04.570 --> 00:19:06.370
and also for our business team here.

00:19:07.330 --> 00:19:09.320
There's one key hire at the
moment that we're looking

00:19:09.320 --> 00:19:12.030
for that we are calling
a revenue engineer.

00:19:12.030 --> 00:19:13.810
And no worries this does nothing have

00:19:13.810 --> 00:19:14.660
to do with engineering.

00:19:14.660 --> 00:19:15.900
So you don't need to be an engineer.

00:19:15.900 --> 00:19:17.870
It's more con consulting business type

00:19:17.870 --> 00:19:19.510
of role where we work together closely.

00:19:19.510 --> 00:19:21.660
First of all, with us as
a senior leadership here

00:19:21.660 --> 00:19:23.300
at Building Radar, but also with some

00:19:23.300 --> 00:19:26.320
of our most senior clients,
most important clients.

00:19:26.320 --> 00:19:28.290
And you would have a key part

00:19:28.290 --> 00:19:32.440
in really shaping the direction
of our company with that.

00:19:32.440 --> 00:19:33.440
So if you would be interested

00:19:33.440 --> 00:19:35.700
we would love to get your
applications for that.

00:19:35.700 --> 00:19:37.410
Just two requirements specifically

00:19:37.410 --> 00:19:38.720
for the revenue engineer role.

00:19:38.720 --> 00:19:40.600
You would need to be based in Munich

00:19:40.600 --> 00:19:43.073
and you would need to
be able to speak German.

00:19:44.370 --> 00:19:45.700
Anthony, big, big, thanks

00:19:45.700 --> 00:19:47.710
for this little conversation we had.

00:19:47.710 --> 00:19:49.520
Also I just learned a couple of new points

00:19:49.520 --> 00:19:50.950
that also didn't knew
about you, which was,

00:19:50.950 --> 00:19:52.630
I think quite fantastic.

00:19:52.630 --> 00:19:53.463
Appreciate your time.

00:19:53.463 --> 00:19:54.680
Thank you so much.

00:19:54.680 --> 00:19:55.990
And let's see, I think we have lots

00:19:55.990 --> 00:19:57.930
of other topics to potentially
explore in the future.

00:19:57.930 --> 00:19:59.160
Maybe also about what it's like

00:19:59.160 --> 00:20:01.850
to be a Chief Product
Officer or things like that.

00:20:01.850 --> 00:20:04.270
So let's see if there's maybe
episode two with Anthony.

00:20:04.270 --> 00:20:05.380
Maybe also let us know the comments

00:20:05.380 --> 00:20:06.903
if you would be interested in that.

00:20:07.830 --> 00:20:08.663
Thank you.

00:20:10.110 --> 00:20:12.710
As always if you took any
value out of this video

00:20:12.710 --> 00:20:14.670
smash the like button
for the YouTube algorithm

